1. Nuclear Fission: Splitting Atoms (Current Technology)

This is the process used in nuclear power plants and atomic bombs. In nuclear fission, a heavy atomic nucleus (typically uranium-235 or plutonium-239) splits into smaller nuclei when struck by a neutron, releasing a large amount of energy in the form of heat.

How it works:

A neutron hits a heavy nucleus (like uranium-235).

This causes the nucleus to become unstable, and it splits into two smaller nuclei (fission products), releasing additional neutrons and energy.

The released neutrons can then go on to cause more fission reactions (a chain reaction).

The energy released is typically in the form of heat, which is used to generate electricity in power plants.

Mass-Energy Conversion:
In this process, the total mass of the products (the smaller nuclei and neutrons) is slightly less than the mass of the original uranium nucleus. The difference in mass (called the mass defect) is converted into energy, according to 
